Follows the logic I explained in my comment:
The operation "evenly spread" means, that every position in the result is either a lit candle (1), an unlit candle (0) or another lit candle (the one you selected!). An operation is mandatory if there is any such moment, that two candles at the same position (with both candidates lit) will at the same moment be light again in an adjacent position. All other positions can be empty.
To solve the second question: How many operations does it take to get to the result?

The number of operations to get from the initial state to the expected present state is equal to the number of dual changes.

Dual change = 2*times an operation is performed

