'''

A segregation (radix sort algorithm) approach produces the worst time complexity. This may be implemented as a recursive-descent based program:

const int base = 10;
const int max_num = 10E8;
vector<int> convert(int num, int pos) {
  vector<int> v;
  for(int d; num; num /= base) {
    v.push_back(num % base);
  }
  reverse(v.begin(), v.end());
  for(auto& d : v) ++d; // normalize
  accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0, [(int res, int c) { return res * base + c; },
                   [&pos](int a, int b) { a /= base; b /= pos; return a + (b * pos); });
  return pos == base - 1 ? v : convert(v[pos], pos + 1);
}

long long dif(int num, vector<int>& best, int x, int y) {
  if(num == 0 || best[num] >= y) return 0;
  auto cur = convert(num, 0);
  return max(dif(prod(cur, best), best, x, y), cur[x] != y ? (long long)y - cur[x]
    : dif(prod(cur, best), best, 0, y) + best.size());
}

long long maxDiff(int num) {
  vector<int> best(max_num, 0);
  best[0] = 1;

  return dif(num, best, 0, 9);
}

A:

It sounds like you are providing an alternate method to which the poster may add comments or suggest corrections. If so, please attach the method below your question as a comment rather than a new answer. Thanks!

Notes:

We have to be really careful with the leading zeros. Whenever you get a zero on the left before the loop begins or when you have zeros on the left side after the second to last pass it means that the resulting string would begin with a zero. We should prevent this from ever occurring and notice that all digits processed the second time would have already been zero the first time if they stay zero.
Your example of 8 as a result of 9 -> 1 -> 1 is actually 9 -> 1 -> 0.
Sadly, the recursive solution is going to be prohibitively slow due to exceeding the maximum recursion depth. And also, it's going to produce exponentially growing intermediate results, which will consume exponentially more memory than the inputs!
The following approach is quite likely to run a lot faster (though still requiring some fine-tuning). I'm going to assume that my assumptions listed below are correct and code up a framework version without actually testing it. However, the assumptions need to be tested on a more representative set of inputs before this code finds its way into production. (Famous Last Words...)

Assumptions:

The number of digits in num is always less than 100.
We can safely ignore leading zeros, because we'll never reach a part of the code that actually touches the final answer (num isn't even going to be nonzero).
There's a reasonable expectation that when we change a digit, it won't change the number of digits and just cycle through from the number with all 9s down to our original number.
(Improvement:) As soon as we realize that a digit would need to change to a 0, we can stop the entire algorithm and return the default value of 0. Imagine the number 109,888. Changing 1 to a zero doesn't affect the outcome, but the 0 digit obviously always has to be 9 to minimize the result.
(Improvement:) Another good reason to pass a set of fixed digits is that the result for a two-digit number with the same repeatable digit can never be any larger than 81, which we can easily write a condition check into the general-case function for any number with as few as two digits.

Code:
The basic framework of this solution involves:

Find the digits that we can swap for the maximum desired effect (fewer than 81 or fewer), and
Find the digits that we must swap to prevent having a leading zero.

We'll embed a helper function into our main function but pass it enough information to make the second version of the function simple. For the first part of our solution, we don't yet know which digits have to change for our result to be nonzero, so we need to call the helper function twice.

