A:

The OP appears not to have any clue how they are supposed to try and solve the task given.
See defining the task given (here again specifically the section Requirements).
The clue given is that they should solve the problem in Java.

The keyboard is key.
The answer is not only found by the main problem, the input data (here actually is just the integer $t$) and the "alphabet" in the problem (here again just the right/up directions).
The OPs failure is as follows

The input data will always be read in a scanner object and this is a thread-safe object. Therefore the task can not be solved in a retry loop where acessing the input data will always take the maximum available time. Please use a better Parallelization technique.

Use of a boolean to stop the solver may seem clever but the time-stamp from scanner.readLine() appears to do the same job more elegant.

The expected output for 21 is not possible in 3 moves with the direction "RUURRRU"

The outfit using a board like in a maze is not the best approach when applying the formulas given from the problem given in the [Link for input data](https://github.com/cbennett/codejam-2017/blob/master/src/sample_input_hl-8.txt#L184-L190)

The problem consists in the fact that for an optimal (lexicographically smaller) answer it is not necessary to take all "steps" for the solution but rather only the path on the actual board.

Practical Answer;
