A zero-based integer x is XOR-strictly less than another zero-based integer y if (x AND y) = 0 and (x OR y) < y.

More formally, the XOR operation between two integers x and y is (x XOR y), which equals x + y unless x and y share the same 1-bit, in which case the result is individually 0. True result is false.

