Given two integers dividend and divisor. Add up all the multiple of divisor which is not greater than dividend.

In other words, add all the 'k' to the dividend such that 'k*divisor' is less than or equal to the dividend and 'k' is a non-negative integer. 'k*divisor' is the closest possible value to the dividend.

At least K frequent elements [1,2,2,3,3,3,4,4,4,4] k=3, then result is 4*3 = 12, 4 comes 3 times and 3 comes 2 times, perfect division.

'''
public class Main {
    public int addUpTillDividend(int dividend, int divisor) {
        int result = 0;
        while(dividend >= divisor) {
            int k = 0;
            while(dividend - (divisor << k << 1) >= 0) {
                k += k;
            }
            result += divisor << k;
            dividend -= divisor << k;
        }
        return result;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Main m = new Main();
        System.out.println(m.addUpTillDividend(10, 3));
    }
}

'''
It seems like, first it adds divisor (3 in the above case) X times (after each iteration) till dividend is greater than or equal to divisor. Then adds divisor X2 times and so on. If it doesn't reach, then it will stop and return the cumulative total. No need to worry about k*divisor is larger than dividend since we've handled higher k values.
You run into with this problem because of 2 things, 1. How to handle the 'K' frequency in the full problem, and 2. How to run the loop properly till dividend is less than or equals to divisor. 
The first one is about how to limit the number of times to loop, and the second one is to make sure we don't get an infinite loop, we will break the loop if the dividend is less than divisor.

